<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext (plain HTTP / ws://) traffic policy.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: React Native's release build sets
  `android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"`, which is a good default for a normal app
  and completely wrong for this one. A self-hosted gateway's server is almost
  always on the local network - `http://192.168.1.5:4000` - and without this the
  release APK installs, launches, and then fails every connection with an error
  that says nothing about TLS. That is hours of someone's evening.

  A `networkSecurityConfig` takes precedence over `usesCleartextTraffic`, so this
  file is what actually decides the policy.

  WHY IT IS SET TO `true` RATHER THAN AN ALLOWLIST: Android's allowlist is by
  DOMAIN. There is no way to express "any RFC1918 address", and we cannot know a
  user's LAN IP at build time. Permitting cleartext broadly is the only thing that
  makes a LAN deployment work at all.

  WHAT THIS COSTS YOU: over plain HTTP, the API key in the WebSocket handshake and
  the bodies of every SMS travel unencrypted. On a home or office LAN, that is
  usually an acceptable risk and it is your call to make. Over the public internet
  it is NOT: put the backend behind TLS and use an `https://` URL, at which point
  the app negotiates `wss://` automatically (see websocketUrl() in src/config.ts)
  and none of this applies.

  The app shows a warning whenever the configured server URL is not https, so this
  trade-off is visible to whoever is making it rather than buried in a manifest.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
